 2476| /*
 2477|  *  tcnd:  Trap on Condition  (#307)
 2478|  *
 2479|  *  arg[0] = the m5 condition mask
 2480|  *  arg[1] = pointer to the source register
 2481|  *  arg[2] = trap vector
 2482|  *
 2483|  *  The instruction was absent from the decoder entirely, so a legal encoding
 2484|  *  reached `goto bad` and stopped the emulator -- reproduced on the luna88k
 2485|  *  rig for both `tcnd ne0,r0,0` and `tcnd lt0,r2,0` before this fix.
 2486|  *
 2487|  *  The condition field is a MASK over four mutually exclusive classes of the
 2488|  *  source register, and the manual gives the bits directly: 8 "maximum
 2489|  *  negative number", 4 "less than zero", 2 "equal to zero", 1 "greater than
 2490|  *  zero" (bit 16 is reserved).  The trap is taken when the register's class
 2491|  *  is among the bits set.  Every entry of the disassembler's own condition
 2492|  *  table agrees with that reading -- ge0 = 3 = eq0|gt0, le0 = 0xe =
 2493|  *  maxneg|lt0|eq0, ne0 = 0xd = maxneg|lt0|gt0 -- which is what makes this a
 2494|  *  derivation rather than a guess.
 2495|  *
 2496|  *  Upstream's abandoned 0.7.1 line took a narrower route: it treated
 2497|  *  `tcnd ne0,r0,x` as a nop, since r0 is never nonzero, and left everything
 2498|  *  else halting.  That is right about the nop and wrong as a fix -- under it
 2499|  *  `tcnd eq0,r0,x` still reaches `goto bad`, and 0 == 0 is exactly the case
 2500|  *  that MUST trap.  Modelling the condition removes the halt for every form,
 2501|  *  and the r0 nop falls out of it for free.
 2502|  *
 2503|  *  The privilege and vector handling is tb0/tb1's, verbatim: user mode may
 2504|  *  not reach the system trap vectors.
